    "turn_idx": 0,
    "turn_text": "Hey everybody, welcome to the Michael Steele podcast. I'm Michael Steele and it's a pleasure to have you back in the neighborhood. Thanks for dropping in. It's always a pleasure when you do. I love it when you do that downloads thing. As you know, catch us on Apple Podcasts. Get your tuning wherever you get your downloads and certainly give us a five star review, share us on social media. Check me out on Twitter at Michael Steele. So I wanted to have a sort of a laser focus in this conversation about women in politics. And I was thinking back when I was talking to a producer about this, about a young woman that I knew back in the day who was stirring up the pot in a little place called Virginia. She would go on to get herself elected in to Congress in 2014 serving two terms representing Virginia's 10th congressional district. She was named as one of the top 10 most effective lawmakers in the 115th Congress by the Center for Effective Lawmaking. She now serves as a senior advisor with Baker Donaldson, where she offers clients decades of her policy experience in the legislative administrative and private sector areas. She is none other than Congressman Barbara Comstock from the great state of Virginia, whom I've known a long, long time. Going back to, probably, when I was a county chairman, certainly when I was a state chairman here in Maryland. And what's exciting about Barbara is that she does the politics, but she has an anchor into what people are going through, how they live their lives, what their experiences are. And as an example of that, Her legislative and cheese include passing legislation to promote women and disadvantaged populations in STEM, as well as expanding research in the technology space. So when people talk about what women do, Barbara, you're a good model of that, how elective leadership can make a difference in a number of areas. And so I wanted to get you on to talk about that. And of course, some of the crazy stuff is going on inside. Republican politics right now. But welcome, welcome to the podcast. It's such a pleasure.",
