    "turn_text": "Very, very different time. So I want to, you know, I think a lot, a lot of the conversations that we hear in, particularly in political space about women is from the democratic side, the progressive left especially, in many instances, even to the exclusion sometimes on, for Democrats of, strong candidates and now elected officials like Mikey Sheryl of New Jersey. We've seen in this cycle a diverse selection of GOP women leaders and we've seen gas flies, crazy. Marjorie Taylor Greene comes to mind. Certainly, I think the preeminent female Republican leader right now is Liz Cheney. When you step back and you then look more broadly at what the landscape looks like, Republicans have so far 127 GOP women who've announced their plans to run for a US House seat, more than twice the 50 women who had declared their candidacy at this point in the 2020 cycle. What do you think attributes to that? What does this crop, because I know you're working with a number of these women, what does it look like? Sound like, feel like.",
